{"title":"Euclidean domain","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <b>Euclidean domain</b> (or <b>Euclidean ring</b>) is a type of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ring\" title=\"Ring\">ring</a> in which the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclidean_algorithm\" title=\"Euclidean algorithm\">Euclidean algorithm</a> can be used.\n</p><p>Formally we say that a ring <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a Euclidean domain if:\n</p>\n<ul><li>It is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integral_domain\" title=\"Integral domain\">integral domain</a>.</li>\n<li>There a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/6/af65f9ae47628121449e3b24602a1e6f2291a3ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N:R\\setminus\\{0\\}\\to \\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"150\" height=\"20\" /> called a <b>Norm</b> such that for all nonzero <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/4/984b759dc432f7dd0f2524100b7a8c182b1244f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b\\in R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"16\" /> there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c2d6446919d147c44ace6e27a3419b72b6eb97a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q,r\\in R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52fd33cd6e3a54109a685dbc485a94792f51d5a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = bq+r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"80\" height=\"16\" /> and either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/2/7b2011e92c5e935110109c760746692367231809.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N(r)&lt;N(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/400365c088499af977542271fc6363b086a63499.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=0$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>Some common examples of Euclidean domains are:\n</p>\n<ul><li>The ring of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integers\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Integers\">integers</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b43768913fd5dd350a34daf1051839ef19642053.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb Z$\" width=\"11\" height=\"12\" /> with norm given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/7/81788c60795198721bd11c7d7a69f07daa572721.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N(a) = |a|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" />.</li>\n<li>The ring of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gaussian_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Gaussian integers (page does not exist)\">Gaussian integers</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/a/0da9b2c73d35e0d39fbace6b7ff4708b92f1791b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb Z[i]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> with norm given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/a/d5a98a0c748d94d104317412ca9e012345b8ccff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N(a+bi) = a^2+b^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"154\" height=\"19\" />.</li>\n<li>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_ring\" title=\"Polynomial ring\">ring of polynomials</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/5/3058cfec73981ccfab32d12bb576d63b2bf97bf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F[x]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> over any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Field\" title=\"Field\">field</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with norm given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/1/f81761cacb79e1c85e546faa7c860c366952f777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N(p) = \\deg p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"103\" height=\"18\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>It can be easily shown through <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite_descent&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Infinite descent (page does not exist)\">infinite descent</a> that any Euclidian domain is also a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Principal_ideal_domain\" title=\"Principal ideal domain\">principal ideal domain</a>. Indeed, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> be any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ideal\" title=\"Ideal\">ideal</a> of a Euclidean domain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and take some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/99028b860e229e02a8ac33e16791335c21705600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a\\in I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> with minimal norm. We claim that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/d/dada0900adc186e00bcd66e6a71c694f4a755fcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I=(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" />. Clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/1/a01f4a896e9fcf400cc9e1192f995f7f10a2b671.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a)\\subseteq I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is an ideal. Now assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/3/3c3477a898dc90285eff226c5a87da72df2af946.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a)\\ne I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> and consider any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad265a71a2dd09de9dbcffb4b60f5dc0a29740a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b\\in I\\setminus (a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" />. Applying the division algorithm we get that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c2d6446919d147c44ace6e27a3419b72b6eb97a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q,r\\in R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/b/d3b2d230bf9daef52ecb0852cfc4effc82661c5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=aq+r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"80\" height=\"16\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/0/c408b27368ee473263e3fb2444d69b2324e57221.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N(r)&lt;N(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" /> (we cannot have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/400365c088499af977542271fc6363b086a63499.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=0$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/b/24b5ff60f220b26d9e1189d07081bed815455b58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b\\not\\in (a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />). But now as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is an ideal, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/43872ffb5297ebe9c79f02dd2c9c3152dac1e0db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b\\in I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/7/207eb3d53e0c7c934f13c10666e88d9a0a73bcf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=b-aq\\in I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" />, contradicting the minimality of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/d/dada0900adc186e00bcd66e6a71c694f4a755fcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I=(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is indeed a principle ideal domain.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclidean_domain&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_domain\">Wikipedia: Euclidean Domain</a></li></ul>\n<ul><li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanRing.html\">Mathworld: Euclidean Ring</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Euclidean_domain&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018083842\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.022 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.037 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 184/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 289/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5798/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.444      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.444      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4631:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018083842 and revision id 32721. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}